60,3,1,talk,Kenji,,Candidate C is on our servers. It might handle the yard devices better.,"{""words"":13,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Mizuki,,Candidate A is a subscription. The renewal data suggests bundled is cheaper than separate.,"{""words"":14,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Asuka,,Candidate D has a regional service team. That solves the unowned support problem.,"{""words"":13,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Rin,,"Candidate B is modular. It allows upgrades in parts, which the dispatch tool needed.","{""words"":14,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Servas,,Candidate C has an engineering team present. That helps with the administrator gap.,"{""words"":13,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Mizuki, A is bundled. That fits the cost data. But is it flexible enough for the yard?","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@Kenji, yes. The board minutes show we need to avoid separate license creep. A is safer.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Rin, B is modular, but the support ownership is still unclear. D has a clear team.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[""Rin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Rin,,"@Asuka, D is hosted. The 2018 data lift was external. Hosted might be safer for data integrity.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Servas,,"@Kenji, C is on-premise. The administrators are leaving. We need someone to stay and teach.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Kenji,,C is built for transport. It knows our calendar. The peak season risk is lower.,"{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Mizuki,,A is a subscription. The operating line is predictable. B has separate licenses that climb.,"{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Asuka,,D is a fixed configuration. The scope creep in the warehouse rules worries me. A is configurable.,"{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Rin,,A has a partner for migration. The 2018 lift was messy. We need a strong partner.,"{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Servas,,"A has a partner for training. The supervisors need deep engagement. C has engineers, but A has trainers.","{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Kenji,,I vote for C. It is built for us and handles the yard reality.,"{""words"":14,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Mizuki,,I vote for A. The cost structure is the most stable and predictable.,"{""words"":13,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Asuka,,I vote for A. The configurable workflows handle the scope changes better than D.,"{""words"":14,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Rin,,"I vote for A. The partner firm handles the migration, reducing the data lift risk.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Servas,,I vote for A. The training partner addresses the administrator gap and supervisor resistance.,"{""words"":14,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
